# Getting Started with Eurostatx

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # To try a local checkout use {:eurostatx, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")} instead
  {:eurostatx, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## Introduction

[Eurostat](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat) is the statistical office of the European Union. Through
its [APIs](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/user-guides/data-browser/api-data-access) you can
access every dataset of the Eurostat database: national accounts (GDP), consumer prices (HICP),
population, labour market, international trade, regional statistics and much more.

The APIs are free and do **not** require an API key, so there is nothing to configure. Let's
alias the modules we'll use and attach the built-in logger so that we can see every request made
to Eurostat:

```elixir
alias Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

require Explorer.DataFrame

Eurostatx.Telemetry.Logger.attach(level: :info)
```

## Datasets and Dimensions

Datasets are identified by their **online data code**, shown next to their title in the Eurostat
website and in the [Data Browser](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/databrowser). We'll start with
`prc_hicp_minr`, the Harmonised Index of Consumer Prices (HICP), the measure of inflation of the
European Central Bank.

A dataset has several **dimensions**. `Eurostatx.Datasets.values/2` lists them, with the codes used
by the dataset and their labels:

```elixir
{:ok, dimensions} = Eurostatx.Datasets.values("prc_hicp_minr")

for dimension <- dimensions do
  %{
    id: dimension["id"],
    name: dimension["name"],
    values: length(dimension["values"]),
    examples: dimension["values"] |> Enum.take(4) |> Enum.map_join(", ", & &1["code"])
  }
end
```

So, to get the annual rate of change (`unit: "RCH_A"`) of the all-items index
(`coicop18: "TOTAL"`) we filter by those codes. Let's see what the units are:

```elixir
dimensions |> Enum.find(&(&1["id"] == "unit")) |> Map.fetch!("values")
```

## The Statistics API

`Eurostatx.Statistics.data/2` queries the Statistics API, which returns
[JSON-stat](https://json-stat.org) with labels in English, French or German. Filters are given by
dimension code, in any order, and several values of a dimension are combined with OR:

```elixir
{:ok, json} =
  Eurostatx.Statistics.data("prc_hicp_minr",
    filters: [geo: "EA", unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: "TOTAL"],
    last_periods: 3
  )

Map.take(json, ["label", "id", "size", "value", "status", "updated"])
```

JSON-stat is a cube: `"value"` holds the observations by position and `"status"` their flags
(`"e"` estimated, `"p"` provisional...). Most of the time you'll want an `Explorer.DataFrame`, with
one row per observation:

```elixir
Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!("prc_hicp_minr",
  filters: [geo: ["EA", "DE"], unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: "TOTAL"],
  last_periods: 3,
  labels: true
)
```

The `date` column has the first day of each period (here, months), so it is ready for charts.

## Wide Data Frames and Charts

With `shape: :wide` you get a `date` column plus one column per series, named after the codes that
vary between them, just like `Fred.Series.observations_as_data_frame/2`:

```elixir
inflation =
  Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!("prc_hicp_minr",
    filters: [geo: ["EA", "DE", "FR", "ES", "IT"], unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: "TOTAL"],
    start_period: "2019-01",
    shape: :wide
  )
```

For charts with several series the long format is more convenient:

```elixir
long =
  Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!("prc_hicp_minr",
    filters: [geo: ["EA", "DE", "FR", "ES", "IT"], unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: "TOTAL"],
    start_period: "2019-01",
    labels: true
  )

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 300, title: "HICP - annual rate of change (%)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(DF.select(long, ["date", "geo_label", "value"]))
|> Vl.mark(:line)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "date", type: :temporal, title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "value", type: :quantitative, title: "%")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "geo_label", type: :nominal, title: nil)
```

## Time Filters

`start_period` and `end_period` take years, periods (`"2024-Q1"`, `"2024-03"`) or `Date`s.
`last_periods` returns the last periods of the dataset and `time` specific periods:

```elixir
Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!("prc_hicp_minr",
  filters: [geo: "EA", unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: "TOTAL"],
  time: ["2024-12", "2025-12"]
)
```

## Labels in Other Languages

Labels are in English by default. Use `language: :fr` or `:de` (or
`config :eurostatx, language: :de`):

```elixir
Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!("prc_hicp_minr",
  filters: [geo: ["DE", "AT"], unit: "RCH_A", coicop18: ["TOTAL", "CP01"]],
  last_periods: 2,
  labels: true,
  language: :de
)
|> DF.select(["geo_label", "coicop18_label", "time", "value"])
```

## Regional Data

The `geo` dimension of regional datasets has countries and regions of the
[NUTS classification](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/nuts). `geo_level` selects a level:

```elixir
"nama_10r_2gdp"
|> Eurostatx.Statistics.data_as_data_frame!(
  filters: [unit: "PPS_EU27_2020_HAB"],
  geo_level: :nuts2,
  last_periods: 1,
  labels: true
)
|> DF.sort_by(desc: value)
|> DF.select(["geo", "geo_label", "time", "value"])
|> DF.head(10)
```

## Errors and Discontinued Datasets

Every function returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, %Eurostatx.Error{}}`:

```elixir
Eurostatx.Statistics.data("not_a_dataset")
```

Invalid options are caught before making any request:

```elixir
Eurostatx.Statistics.data("prc_hicp_minr", time: 2024, last_periods: 3)
```

Beware of **discontinued datasets**: when a classification changes, Eurostat publishes new datasets
and stops updating the old ones, which keep answering with their historical data. The HICP based on
COICOP 1999 (`prc_hicp_manr`) was replaced by the one based on ECOICOP ver.2 (`prc_hicp_minr`) in
2026. Check the latest period of a dataset with `Eurostatx.Datasets.summary/1`:

```elixir
for dataset <- ["prc_hicp_manr", "prc_hicp_minr"] do
  {:ok, dataflow} = Eurostatx.Datasets.get(dataset)
  Map.take(Eurostatx.Datasets.summary(dataflow), ["id", "label", "latest_period", "data_updated_at"])
end
```

Head to the *Exploring Datasets* notebook to learn how to find the data you need.
